  "body": "  Graph the function on the interval .   What is the -intercept of this graph? The graph has two -intercepts; use algebra to find them.  You can also find an -intercept using the computer. The idea is to   magnify the graph near the intercept until you can determine as many decimal places in the coordinate as you want. For a start, graph the function on the interval . You should be able to see that the graph on your computer monitor crosses the -axis somewhere around .7. Regraph on the interval . You should then be able to determine that the -intercept lies between .70 and .71. This means .7…; that is, you know the location of the -intercept to one decimal place of accuracy.   Regraph on the interval to get two decimal places of accuracy in the location of the -intercept. Continue this process until you have at least 7 places of accuracy. What is the -intercept?   "
