  "number": "10.4.3.7",
  "title": "",
  "body": "   Legendre’s differential equation  arises in many physical problems—for example, in quantum mechanics, where its solutions are used to describe certain orbits of the electron in a hydrogen atom. In that context, the parameter is called the angular momentum of the electron; it must be either an integer or a half-integer (i.e., a number like 3\/2). Quantum theory gets its name from the fact that numbers like the angular momentum of the electron in the hydrogen atom are “quantized\", that is, they cannot have just any value, but must be a multiple of some “quantum\"—in this case, the number 1\/2.   Find power series solutions of Legendre’s equation.   Quantization of angular momentum has an important consequence. Specifically, when is an integer it is possible for a series solution to stop—that is, to be a polynomial. For example, when and the series solution is just —all higher order coefficients turn out to be zero. Find polynomial solutions to Legendre’s equation for and (consider or ). These solutions are called, naturally enough, Legendre polynomials .   "
