  "number": "11.7",
  "title": "Improper Integrals",
  "body": " Improper Integrals      The Lifetime of Light Bulbs    Ordinary light bulbs are supposed to burn about 700 hours,  The lifetime of a light bulb is unpredictable  but of course some last longer while others burn out more quickly. It is impossible to know, in advance, the lifetime of a particular bulb you might buy, but it is possible to describe what happens to a large batch of bulbs.  Suppose we take a batch of 1000 light bulbs, start them burning at the same time, and note how long it takes each one to burn out. Let Then might have a graph that looks like this:      In this example, , so about half the bulbs burned out before 400 hours. Furthermore, all but a few have burned out by 1250 hours.  Manufacturers are very concerned  The burnout rate  about the way the lifetime of light bulbs varies. They study the output of their factories on a regular basis. It is more common, though, for them to talk about the rate  at which bulbs burn out. The rate varies over time, too. In fact, in terms of , is just the derivative However, if we start with the rate , then we get as the integral This is yet another consequence of  Lifetime is the integral of burnout rate…  the fundamental theorem of calculus. The integral expression is quite handy. For example, the fraction of bulbs that burn out between hours and hours is   We can even use the integral to say that all the bulbs burn out eventually: In practice is the average burnout rate  …but there is no upper limit to the lifetime  for many batches of light bulbs, so we can’t identify the precise moment when becomes 1. All we can really say is This is called an improper integral ,  An integral is improper if the domain of integration is infinite  because it cannot be calculated directly: its domain of integration is infinite. By definition, its value is obtained as a limit of ordinary integrals:   The normal density function of probability theory provides us with another example of an improper integral. In a simple form, the function itself is   (Recall, .) If is any normally distributed quantity whose average value is 0, then the probability that a randomly chosen value of lies between the numbers and is Since the probability that lies somewhere   The normal probability distribution involves an improper integral  on the -axis is 1, we have This is an improper integral, and its value is defined by the limit In the exercises you will have a chance to evaluate this integral.    Evaluating Improper Integrals    An integral with an infinite domain of integration is  An integral is also improper if its integrand becomes infinite  only one kind of improper integral. A second kind has a finite domain of integration, but the integrand becomes infinite on that domain. For example, are both improper in this sense. In both cases, the integrand becomes infinite as . Because the difficulty lies at the endpoint 0, we define More generally, is an improper integral if becomes infinite at some point in the interval . In that case we define In effect, we avoid the bad spot but creep up on it in the limit.  Indefinite integrals—that is,  Antiderivatives help find improper integrals  antiderivatives—can be a great help in evaluating improper integrals. Here are some examples.   [[EX:1]].  We can evaluate by noting first that . Therefore and     [[EX:2]].  To evaluate , we use the indefinite integral Thus By direct calculation (using a graphing package, for instance) we can find therefore   You should not assume that an improper integral always has a finite value, though. Consider the next example.   [[EX:3]] .   This is forced because , which you can see from the graph of the logarithm function.     Exercises    Find the value of each of the following improper integrals. (The value may be .)                                     Use the reduction formula for you found on page  to find the exact value of     The normal density function   The next two questions concern the improper integral of the normal density function defined on page . The goal is to determine the value of this integral.    First, use RIEMANN to estimate the value of when has the different values 1, 10, 100, and 1000. On the basis of these results, estimate This gives one estimate of the value of the improper integral.     To construct a second estimate, begin by sketching the graph of the normal density function on an interval centered at the origin. Use the graph to argue that and therefore    Now consider the accumulation function We want to find . According to the fundamental theorem of calculus, satisfies the initial value problem Use a differential equation solver (e.g., PLOT) to graph the solution to this problem. From the graph determine    Does your results in part (b) and question 2 agree? Do they agree with the value the text claims for the improper integral. (Remember, the value is the probability that a randomly chosen number will lie somewhere on the number line between and .)     The gamma function     The factorial function is defined for a positive integer by the formula For example, , , , , and . The factorial function is used often in diverse mathematical contexts, but its use is sometimes limited by the fact that it is defined only for positive integers. How might the function be defined on an expanded domain, so that we could deal with expressions like , for example? The gamma function answers this question.  The gamma function  is defined by the improper integral Notice that is the active variable in this integral. While the integration is being performed, is treated as a constant; for the integral to converge, we need .    Show that .     Using integration by parts, show that . You may use the fact that   The property makes the gamma function like the factorial function, because Notice there is a slight difference, though. We explore this now.     Using the property , calculate , , , , and . On the basis of this evidence, fill in the blank: Using this relation, give a computable meaning to the expression  .     Estimate the value of . (Exercises 2 and 3 above offer two ways to estimate the value of an improper integral.)     In fact, exactly. You can show this by employing several of the techniques developed in this chapter. Start with    Make the substitution and show that the integral becomes    From exercise 4 you know (Check this.) Now, using some algebra, show .   Compare your estimate for from exercise 7 with the exact value .     Determine the exact values of and .   In exercise 6 you gave a meaning to the expression ; can you now give it an exact value?      "
